GitHub Actions Architecture

Today I explored the core building blocks that make GitHub Actions work behind the scenes.

🔹 Events
Events are triggers that start workflows.
 Examples:
Code push
Pull request
Manual trigger
They tell GitHub when to run automation.

🔹 Workflows
Workflows are YAML files stored in .github/workflows/.
 They define:
When automation runs
What jobs should execute
Each repo can have multiple workflows.

🔹 Jobs
Jobs are a set of steps that run on the same runner.
Can run sequentially or in parallel
Can depend on other jobs

🔹 Steps
Steps are individual tasks inside a job.
 Examples:
Checkout code
Run commands
Use actions
Steps execute top to bottom.

🔹 Runners
Runners are machines that execute jobs.
GitHub-hosted runners
Self-hosted runners
They provide the environment for CI/CD.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Events start automation.
 Workflows define the process.
 Runners execute DevOps pipelines.
